msgid "Declare permissions and earn trust"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"*Say what the code needs and why, in the words the person reads before "
"installing it; then let a review answer for it.*"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A Python plugin runs with the powers of the user who installed it, as any"
" program does. EMStudio cannot shut it in a cage on a personal computer, "
"so trust comes from what is **declared** and from who **read** the code. "
"Your part, as the author, is to declare honestly and briefly."
msgstr ""

msgid "What the envelope declares"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Permissions** say what the code does on the computer, each with its "
"**reason**: ``files`` (it reads or writes files), ``network`` (it talks "
"to the network), ``clipboard``, ``processes`` (it starts other programs)."
" The reason is a short sentence, at most 64 characters, without a final "
"full stop, written for the person who will read it in the panel **Code** "
"under **Permissions, and why:**."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"**Capabilities** say what the code does with a study: ``read-graph``, "
"``write-graph``, and the others of :doc:`envelope`. A write without its "
"capability is refused at the moment it is attempted."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``writes`` says, command by command, whether it changes the study. A "
"command declared ``writes = false`` cannot write at all: the library "
"refuses it."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"``hosts`` says where the code runs: ``emstudio``, ``blender`` (EM Tools),"
" ``server`` (a StratiGraph node, in a closed space)."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Declare what the code does, nothing more: every permission the person "
"reads is a question you ask."
msgstr ""

msgid "The question of trust"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Before installing a plugin, EMStudio shows the panel **Code**, marked "
"**NOT VERIFIED** when no review covers it: the permissions with their "
"reasons, what it can do, where its commands appear, and the sentence **A "
"Python plugin has your user's powers on this machine.** The answer — "
"**Install everything**, **Data only**, **Cancel** — is the person's "
"trust."
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The question comes back only when a new version **asks for more**: a new "
"permission, a new capability, another origin. So keep the declarations "
"stable: an update that only fixes the code installs without asking again."
msgstr ""

msgid "Dependencies travel in the archive"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"Nothing is downloaded with ``pip`` when a plugin is installed. A library "
"the code needs travels in the archive as a **wheel**, one for every "
"platform declared in ``[code].platforms`` (a pure-Python wheel serves all"
" of them): the person installs exactly the bytes the repository checked."
msgstr ""

msgid "The development folder is yours"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"The development folder loads code without the question, because the "
"person who writes it is the person who runs it. Its acts are marked "
"**development, not verified** in the menus and in the record of the "
"study."
msgstr ""

msgid "Earn a review"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"A **review** is a person's reading of the code. In the official index it "
"is a line of ``reviewed.json`` in em-extensions: the fingerprint of the "
"code section, who read it, in which role, and when. A plugin is reviewed "
"by the editor or by the curators of a community package that requires it;"
" its author does not review it alone. A code whose fingerprint is "
"reviewed installs without the question and is marked **reviewed by … on "
"…**."
msgstr ""

msgid "To make a review easy:"
msgstr ""

msgid "keep the code short and readable, and the permissions few;"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"write the files the code makes in ``ctx.data_dir``, its own folder, and "
"stamp them (see :doc:`stamp-derived-files`);"
msgstr ""

msgid ""
"write every change through ``em.write``, which records the plugin in the "
"act;"
msgstr ""

msgid "add tests that run without EMStudio (see :doc:`test-and-validate`)."
msgstr ""

